{"cells":[{"kind":1,"value":"# TEST WEEK 3","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":1,"value":"## 1. Define the following table in a Q session:\n```\ntable: ([]a: 1 til 5; b: 2 til 5; c: 3 + til 5)","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"table: ([]a: 1 + til 5; b: 2 + til 5; c: 3 + til 5);\ntable","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"a b c\r\n-----\r\n1 2 3\r\n2 3 4\r\n3 4 5\r\n4 5 6\r\n5 6 7\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"### a. Write a query which selects the data from columns a and b in \"table\" where c is greater than or equal to 5, in functional form.","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"'2026.11.17T11:20:22.026 #\r\n  [1]  ### a. Write a query which selects the data from columns a and b in \"table\" where c is greater than or equal to 5, in functional form.\r\n       ^\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":2,"value":"?[`table;enlist ((';~:;<);`c;5); 0b;()]","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"a b c\r\n-----\r\n3 4 5\r\n4 5 6\r\n5 6 7\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"### b. Why might one need to use functional form?","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":1,"value":"```\nTo pass in arguments in place of fixed table and column names","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":1,"value":"## 2. Write a function which generates a table of 100 random numbers between –5 and 5 and a column which indicates whether the number is positive, negative or zero.","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":1,"value":"100?5","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"func:   {[]\n            a:100?(-5 + til 11);\n            b: {[a]$[0 = a;`zero; 0>a; `negative;`positive]} each a ;\n            myTab::   ([]a:a;b:b);\n            :myTab};\n\nmyTab: func[];\nmyTab","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"a  b       \r\n-----------\r\n-1 negative\r\n-4 negative\r\n4  positive\r\n2  positive\r\n-3 negative\r\n5  positive\r\n-5 negative\r\n-1 negative\r\n3  positive\r\n-5 negative\r\n-3 negative\r\n-3 negative\r\n0  zero    \r\n4  positive\r\n3  positive\r\n1  positive\r\n-4 negative\r\n1  positive\r\n3  positive\r\n-2 negative\r\n..\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"### 3. Write a function that takes two dates as arguments and calculates the number of Sundays between them (including both dates).","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"sundayList: {[startDate;endDate]\n                dateList:   startDate + til 1 + abs(endDate - startDate);\n                dateList where 1 = dateList mod 7;\n                :dateList};\n\n\nsundayList[2024.01.01;2027.01.01]","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"2024.01.01 2024.01.02 2024.01.03 2024.01.04 2024.01.05 2024.01.06 2024.01.07 ..\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"## 4.  Create the following table, tab in your q session - note the null values at rows 5 and 11.\n```\ntab: ([]a:til 15; b: 15#(til 5); 0N)","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"tab: ([]a:til 15; b: 15#(til 5),0N)\n\ntab","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"a  b\r\n----\r\n0  0\r\n1  1\r\n2  2\r\n3  3\r\n4  4\r\n5   \r\n6  0\r\n7  1\r\n8  2\r\n9  3\r\n10 4\r\n11  \r\n12 0\r\n13 1\r\n14 2\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"### a Where there is no b value in the table, add in the number 10.","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"tab:    update b:10 from tab where null b;","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"### b. Add a third column, c which contains the following value from column a – where no value is available, 100 should be placed in the column.","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"tab: update c: next a from tab;\ntab: update c: 100 from tab where null c;\ntab","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"a  b  c  \r\n---------\r\n0  0  1  \r\n1  1  2  \r\n2  2  3  \r\n3  3  4  \r\n4  4  5  \r\n5  10 6  \r\n6  0  7  \r\n7  1  8  \r\n8  2  9  \r\n9  3  10 \r\n10 4  11 \r\n11 10 12 \r\n12 0  13 \r\n13 1  14 \r\n14 2  100\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"### c. Finally, add a d column which expresses whether or not the a and b values are the same.","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"tab:    update d: `different from tab where a<>b;\ntab:    update d: `same from tab where a=b;\ntab","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"a  b  c   d        \r\n-------------------\r\n0  0  1   same     \r\n1  1  2   same     \r\n2  2  3   same     \r\n3  3  4   same     \r\n4  4  5   same     \r\n5  10 6   different\r\n6  0  7   different\r\n7  1  8   different\r\n8  2  9   different\r\n9  3  10  different\r\n10 4  11  different\r\n11 10 12  different\r\n12 0  13  different\r\n13 1  14  different\r\n14 2  100 different\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":1,"value":"## 5.  Come up with two separate functions that will find the factorial of a number. Each function should use a different method i.e adverbs, keywords etc. One of your functions must also display every factorial up to the input, for example:\n```\nf1[10]\n3628800\n\nf2[10]\n1 2 6 24 120 720 5040 40320 362880 3628800","languageId":"markdown","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"value":"factorial1: {prd[1 + til x]};\n\nfactorial1[10]","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"3628800\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]},{"kind":2,"value":"factorial2: {(*\\)(1 + til x)};\n\nfactorial2[10]","languageId":"q","outputs":[{"items":[{"data":"1 2 6 24 120 720 5040 40320 362880 3628800\r\n","mime":"text/plain"}]}]}]}